:title("C# (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a C# subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The subset matches csharp-interpreter.abnf: 'using' directives (resolved against the
runtime builtins), 'namespace X.Y { ... }' blocks (unwrapped), classes with instance and
static fields (with initializers), instance and static methods (block bodied and
expression bodied '=> e;'), constructors (with an optional ': base(...)' / ': this(...)'
header that is parsed and skipped), single inheritance ('class B : A' inherits fields and
methods, overrides with dynamic dispatch, runs the implicit base constructor, and
'base.M()' calls; a base class must appear before its subclasses), object creation with
'new', arrays (new int[n], new int[]{...}, a.Length, indexing), List<T> (new List<T>(),
.Add, [i], .Count, .Contains, foreach), Dictionary<K,V> (new Dictionary<K,V>(), d[k]
get/set, .Count, .ContainsKey, .Keys, .Values), the 'foreach (var x in coll)' loop, 'var'
and typed locals/params/returns (parsed; dynamic semantics), string concatenation with +,
'$\"...{expr}...\"' interpolation, the String members .Length, .Substring, .IndexOf and
.Equals, Console.WriteLine / Console.Write mapped to the print builtin, if/else, while,
do-while, both for forms, the colon switch on int or string (fallthrough with break,
stacked labels, default), break/continue, return, assignment (also += -= *= /= %=),
postfix ++/--, the ternary operator, || && with short circuit, == != < > <= >= and
+ - * / % (int arithmetic is 32 bit, / truncates) and unary - !.

Also modelled: char as an integral value that renders as its glyph (with the C# escapes
and the (char)/(int)/(byte) conversions), the full numeric literal set (leading-dot reals,
hex, binary, digit groups, u/l/f/d/m suffixes), verbatim and raw string literals,
interpolation with alignment and the D format specifier, typeof / nameof / sizeof /
default, nullable values with the null-coalescing, null-conditional and null-forgiving
operators and .HasValue / .Value, nested types, enums (with member initializers over
earlier members), structs (value copy on a struct-typed declaration, and a zeroed
default), interfaces with default members and explicit implementations, abstract members,
extension methods (a static method whose first parameter carries this), unqualified calls
to members of the enclosing class, using statements and using declarations with Dispose in
reverse order, local functions, lambdas with typed parameters, default values and a params
tail, anonymous methods and anonymous types, braced array initializers, and object
creation as the head of a member chain.

Also modelled: labels and goto (including goto case / goto default, which re-enter the
enclosing switch), the throw EXPRESSION, the BCL exception types (a program may throw new
Exception(...) without declaring one), named and optional arguments (a skipped parameter
takes its default), collection expressions with a spread element, the index-from-end
operator and range slicing, delegate { ... } without a parameter list, unsafe blocks,
implicitly typed and sized-with-initializer arrays, nested and indexed member
initializers, and explicitly implemented properties.

Also modelled: tuples (a tuple type, positional and named elements) and deconstruction
('var (a, b) = e', '(a, b) = e', a custom Deconstruct(out ...) and a record's positional
members); the pattern grammar (constant, relational, type and declaration, var, discard,
property, positional and list patterns, combined with and / or / not) behind 'is', behind
the switch EXPRESSION and behind the case labels of the switch STATEMENT, both with their
'when' guards; records ('record R(int X);', 'record class', 'record struct') with
deconstruction and the 'with' expression; delegates and events (method group conversion,
anonymous methods, multicast '+=', and '-=' removing the last matching invocation, exactly
as Delegate.Remove does); and LAZY iterator blocks ('yield return' / 'yield break') with
foreach, GetEnumerator / MoveNext / Current - an endless iterator that a foreach breaks
out of terminates.

Records and tuples compare BY VALUE, member by member and recursively, so a record whose
field is itself a record or a tuple compares by value too; every other class keeps
reference equality, and a user-declared 'operator ==' still wins over both.

Also modelled: an event that manages its own subscription list
('event H E { add { ... } remove { ... } }'), whose accessors 'E += h' / 'E -= h' call with
the handler itself.

Widened surface (parsed so a whole C# file is accepted): generics, nullable and array type
suffixes are parsed and dropped; modifier keywords are parsed and ignored. A delegate type
declaration is accepted and dropped (a delegate value is the closure it names); so are a
finalizer ('~X() { }' - nothing here collects), 'extern alias X;' and the 'X::Name' alias
qualifier it enables. LINQ query expressions are accepted and reported as not implemented;
-warn-unsupported downgrades that to a warning so call graphs / CFGs / traces still work on
partly understood files.

An iterator body is an ordinary IR function whose closure is wrapped by js_genfn, so it
runs on its own goroutine and js_yield is a suspending handshake - these are REAL
generators (the lowering languages/python-to-llvm-ir.abnf uses).

Not modelled exactly: async members run synchronously (await is the identity on its
operand), which is a documented approximation rather than a gap - the async machinery has
no scheduler here. A bare type-name case label ('case IShape:', with no binding) is read as
a constant, because telling a type from a constant needs name resolution; the binding form
('case IShape s:') and every built-in type name are read as patterns. Out and ref
parameters are ordinary locals, read back only by a deconstruction.

Fields live directly on the instance object (js_get/js_set), methods become own IR
functions jsf_N(env, args) with the instance prepended to the arguments, stored on the
class descriptor object; calls dispatch through the js_mcall external. Lists compile to the
handle array runtime, dictionaries to js_dict handles (indexing via js_pyget/js_pyset). A
constructor always exists (a default one is generated) and first applies the field
initializers. The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args): it builds Console, the class
descriptors, then calls Program.Main; the process exit code is Main's return value.
The same language with the same semantics is interpreted directly by
csharp-interpreter.abnf.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
// C# whitespace is C's - blanks plus // and /* */ - PLUS the preprocessor. A
// directive line is trivia as far as the parser is concerned, so it is skipped
// wherever a comment would be. That is the one lexical difference from
// lib/cstyle-comments.abnf, and it is why this grammar carries its own copy of
// that fragment instead of including it: the shared lib stays untouched and no
// other language is affected (the precedent is commit 0ed59ab, which did the
// same for an Id that had to differ).
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n" | Comment | PPDirective } ;
Comment     = LineComment | BlockComment ;
LineComment = "//" :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
BlockComment = "/*" :whitespace() { NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A preprocessor directive (ECMA-334 6.5): #define / #undef, #if / #elif /
// #else / #endif, #line, #region / #endregion, #pragma, #error / #warning,
// #nullable, #checksum. '#' is not an operator in C#, so anything from a '#' to
// the end of its line is a directive and is skipped like a comment.
//
// CONDITIONAL COMPILATION IS NOT EVALUATED: the directives vanish and the text of
// EVERY branch is parsed. That is deliberate and it is the honest limit of this
// approach - two branches that each declare the same member both reach the
// runtime, and an #if guarding a syntactically incomplete fragment still has to
// parse. It is right for what this front end does (it recognises syntax; it does
// not model build configurations), and it is what makes a file that merely wraps
// a #region or a #pragma around ordinary code parse at all.
PPDirective = "#" :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = { ExternAlias } { Using } { TopDecl } ;

// A 'using' directive (also 'using static' and the 'using Alias = Path;' form) is
// resolved against the runtime builtins (core.stdlibImports); one that does not resolve
// aborts the compile, unless -warn-imports downgrades it to a warning. It pushes no
// statement, so buildMain never sees it.
Using       <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = [ "global" KwEnd ] KwUsing [ "unsafe" KwEnd ] [ "static" KwEnd ]
              [ Id "=" ] UsingTarget ";" ;
// The target of a using-ALIAS may be a constructed type ('using L = List<string>;',
// 'using A = N.M<double>;'), which a plain QualName cannot spell. The type arguments
// are dropped, like every other generic here, so the name that reaches resolveImport
// is the same dotted path the non-generic form would have produced.
UsingTarget <~~ push(csStripTargs(stripWs(up.in))) ~~>
            = Id [ "::" Id ] [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } [ "." "*" ] ;
QualName    <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Id [ "::" Id ] { "." Id } [ "." "*" ] ;

// A stray ';' after a type declaration (`class F { };`, `enum E { A };`) is legal and
// means nothing - ECMA-334 13.7 lets a `;` stand as a whole namespace member.
TopDecl     = ExternAlias | TopAttr | Namespace | FileNs | UnsupType | TypeDecl | TopSemi ;
TopSemi     = ";" ;
// 'extern alias X;': an assembly alias. There are no assemblies here, so it is parsed and
// dropped; the 'X::Name' references it enables are read as plain qualified names.
ExternAlias = "extern" KwEnd "alias" KwEnd Id ";" ;
// The type declarations that are genuinely modelled.
TypeDecl    = ClassDecl | EnumDecl ;

// A standalone attribute section at file scope ('[assembly: ...]' / '[module: ...]'):
// accepted and ignored (it pushes no declaration thunk).
TopAttr     = AttrSection ;
// A file scoped namespace ('namespace X.Y;'): a no-op marker; the declarations that
// follow it are treated as top level (matching how a block namespace is unwrapped). The
// name QualName pushes is discarded so it never reaches buildMain as a declaration.
FileNs      <~~ pop() ~~> = KwNamespace QualName ";" ;

// namespace X.Y { ... }: the inner declaration thunks are collected into one sequence.
Namespace   <~~ push(makeNamespace(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNamespace QualName "{" { Using | TopDecl } "}" ;

ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ ClassMods ] ClassKw ClassName [ ClassTPs ] [ PrimaryCtor ]
              [ ":" BaseSpec [ SkipParen ] { "," Type [ SkipParen ] } ] { WhereClause } ClassBody ;
// A record may stand without a body ('record R(int X);').
ClassBody   = "{" { Member } "}" | ";" ;
// The modifiers before a type declaration. Only 'partial' is behavioral: the members of
// every part of a partial class are merged into one class (see makeClassStmt).
ClassMods   <~~ push({cmods: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = LeadMods ;
// The type-parameter names of a generic type ('class Pair<TA, TB>'). They are kept so a
// 'new TB()' inside the body can be resolved through the type arguments the instance was
// created with (see makeNew / csTypeFactory).
ClassTPs    <~~ push({tparams: csTypeArgs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = SkipAngle ;
// A primary constructor ('class C(int n) { ... }'): the parameters become instance
// members of the class, so the class body may name them unqualified.
PrimaryCtor <~~ push({primary: pop()}) ~~>
            = ParamList ;
// The first base type. A qualified name ('B.Inner') keeps its last segment, which is the
// simple name every type is registered under.
BaseSpec    <~~ push({ext: csLastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
            = Id [ "::" Id ] [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
// 'class' or 'struct': a struct is built exactly like a class, and additionally copies
// on a declaration whose declared type names it (C# value semantics) and has a zeroed
// default instance.
ClassKw     <~~ push({ckind: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = RecordKw | ( "class" | "struct" | "interface" ) KwEnd ;
// A record ('record R(int X);', 'record class', 'record struct'). It is built exactly
// like the class / struct it names: the positional parameters are a primary constructor,
// so they already become members. What a record adds is value equality, deconstruction
// from those parameters, and the 'with' expression - see buildClassFrom.
RecordKw    = "record" KwEnd [ ( "class" | "struct" ) KwEnd ] ;
// The class name, and the class the enclosing member bodies belong to (curClassName, for
// 'base.M()'): pushed on a stack so a nested class restores its outer name when it ends.
ClassName   <~~ push(up.in); classStack.push(curClassName); curClassName = up.in ~~>
            = Id ;

// A delegate declaration is legal at file scope AND in a class body; it is listed here
// as well, so a nested one does not fall through to Method (which would then demand a
// body).
// MemberSemi is the stray ';' between members - after a nested type declaration
// (`enum E { A, B };`) it is legal and means nothing, exactly like TopSemi above.
Member      = NestedType | NestedUnsup | DelegateDecl | OperatorDecl | Finalizer | Ctor
            | Method | AbstractMethod | IndexerDecl | Property | Field | MemberSemi ;
MemberSemi  = ";" ;

// A finalizer ('~X() { ... }'). There is no garbage collector to run it, so it is parsed
// and dropped - it pushes nothing, and the class body simply skips it.
Finalizer   = [ LeadMods ] "~" Id ParamList ( Block | ";" ) ;

// An overloaded operator ('public static Vec operator +(Vec a, Vec b)', also the unary
// forms) and a user conversion ('public static implicit operator Vec(int n)'). Both are
// static members of the declaring type; they become '__op1_<sym>' / '__op2_<sym>' and
// '__conv_<T>' members, which binOp / makeNeg / makeCast / a typed declaration consult.
OperatorDecl = ConvDecl | BinOpDecl ;
ConvDecl    <~~ push(makeConvOp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods ConvKind KwOperator CType ParamList MethodBody ;
ConvKind    = ( "implicit" | "explicit" ) KwEnd ;
CType       <~~ push({cty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
BinOpDecl   <~~ push(makeOperatorDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType KwOperator OpSym ParamList MethodBody ;
// Longer symbols first, so '++' is never read as '+'.
OpSym       <~~ push({opsym: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = "<<" | ">>" | "==" | "!=" | "<=" | ">=" | "++" | "--" | "true" KwEnd
            | "false" KwEnd | "+" | "-" | "*" | "/" | "%" | "&" | "|" | "^" | "~" | "!"
            | "<" | ">" ;
KwOperator  = "operator" KwEnd ;

// An indexer ('public int this[int i] { get { ... } }'): its accessors become the
// class' __index / __setindex members, which the index read and the index assignment
// consult when the receiver is an instance of a class that declares one.
// An indexer. The name may be qualified with the interface it explicitly implements
// ('int IFoo<T>.this[int i] { get { ... } }'); there is one indexer per class here,
// so the interface name is parsed and dropped. The body may be a block of accessors
// or an expression, which is a get-only accessor - the same pair Property allows.
IndexerDecl <~~ push(makeIndexer(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType [ IfaceQual ] KwThis IdxParams ( PropAccs | PropArrow ) ;
// Each segment carries its OWN trailing dot. Written as `... { "." Id } "."` the
// repetition is greedy and swallows the `.this`, and then the final "." has nothing
// left to match, so the whole optional silently matched empty and `this` was never
// reached.
IfaceQual   = Id [ "::" Id ] [ SkipAngle ] "." { Id [ SkipAngle ] "." } ;
IdxParams   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ Param { "," Param } ] "]" ;

// A type declared inside a class body is a real type: its declaration runs with the
// outer one's, it registers under its simple name, and the outer class descriptor gets a
// member of that name so 'Outer.Inner' resolves too.
NestedType  <~~ push(makeNested(pop())) ~~>
            = TypeDecl ;

// A constructor: name matches the class; an optional ': base(...)' / ': this(...)'
// header is parsed and skipped (the implicit base constructor already ran). The body is
// a block or an expression bodied '=> e;' form.
Ctor        <~~ push(makeCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ CtorInit ] CtorBody ;
CtorInit    = ":" ( KwBase | KwThis ) SkipParen ;
CtorBody    = Block | CtorArrow ;
CtorArrow   <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~> = "=>" Expression ";" ;

// A method. The name may be qualified with the interface it explicitly implements
// ('string ITag.Tag()'); only the last segment is kept, so the member is reachable under
// its plain name (the interface view is not modelled separately).
Method      <~~ push(makeMethod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods Type MethodName [ SkipAngle ] ParamList { WhereClause } MethodBody ;
// csSimpleName drops the type arguments of both the interface and the method itself
// ('IEnumerable<Foo>.GetEnumerator', 'Echo<T>') and keeps the last segment.
MethodName  <~~ push(csSimpleName(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
MethodBody  = Block | ArrowBody ;
// A member declared without a body: an abstract method, or an interface method that has
// no default implementation. It contributes no member, so a subclass' override is what
// the dispatch finds.
AbstractMethod <~~ push(makeAbstract(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods Type MethodName [ SkipAngle ] ParamList { WhereClause } ";" ;
ArrowBody   <~~ push(makeArrowBody(pop())) ~~>
            = "=>" Expression ";" ;

// A property. An automatically implemented accessor pair ('{ get; set; }', 'init' and
// 'required' included) makes the property a plain FIELD of the class - so an object
// initializer, a read and a write all work unchanged. An accessor with a body instead
// becomes a '__get_<name>' / '__set_<name>' member, which the field read (core.getField)
// and the assignment path consult when the object carries no member of that name.
Property    <~~ push(makeProperty(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType PropName ( PropAccs [ PropInit ] | PropArrow ) ;
// The name may be qualified with the interface it explicitly implements
// ('int I.P { get { ... } }'); only the last segment is kept.
PropName    <~~ push({pname: csSimpleName(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
PropAccs    <~~ push({accs: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { Accessor } "}" ;
Accessor    = GetAcc | SetAcc | AddAcc | RemoveAcc ;
GetAcc      <~~ push(makeAccessor("get", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods "get" KwEnd AccBody ;
SetAcc      <~~ push(makeAccessor("set", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods ( "set" | "init" ) KwEnd AccBody ;
// An accessor without a body ('get;') is the automatic one.
// The accessors of an EVENT that manages its own subscription list
// ('event EventHandler E { add { ... } remove { ... } }'). They become '__add_E' /
// '__remove_E' members, which 'E += h' / 'E -= h' call with the handler.
AddAcc      <~~ push(makeAccessor("add", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods "add" KwEnd AccBody ;
RemoveAcc   <~~ push(makeAccessor("remove", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods "remove" KwEnd AccBody ;
AccBody     = ";" | Block | ArrowBody ;
PropInit    <~~ push({pinit: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" InitVal ";" ;
PropArrow   <~~ push({parrow: pop()}) ~~>
            = ArrowBody ;

// A field declaration may declare SEVERAL names ('public int X, Y;'). Each declarator
// becomes a field of its own; buildClass flattens the group before the members are read.
Field       <~~ push(makeFieldGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType FieldDecl { "," FieldDecl } ";" ;
// The '[n]' is the FIXED-SIZE BUFFER declarator ('public fixed int data[8];'), an
// unsafe-only form. The length is a constant expression that nothing here reads, so
// it is skipped rather than parsed, and the field is an ordinary one. The grammar
// does not insist on the 'fixed' modifier: modifiers are collected generically, and
// refusing the declarator without it would buy nothing this front end can use.
FieldDecl   <~~ push({fdecl: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ FixedDim ] [ "=" InitVal ] ;
FixedDim    = "[" { FixedDimCh } "]" ;
FixedDimCh  = "\x00"..."\x5c" | "\x5e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// An initializer value. The braced array initializer ('int[] a = {1, 2};') is only legal
// here and in a local declaration, so it is not part of Expression.
InitVal     = ArrLit | Expression ;
ArrLit      <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" [ ArrElem { "," ArrElem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
ArrElem     = ArrLit | Expression ;

// Modifiers before a member; ModWord keeps the text so 'static' stays detectable. An
// attribute section here (e.g. '[Obsolete] static int F') is accepted and dropped.
Mods        <~~ push({mods: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = { ModWord | AttrSection } ;
ModWord     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = ModKw ;
FType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;

// A parameter list. 'this' on the first parameter marks an extension method and 'params'
// marks the variadic tail, so both modifiers leave a marker; the rest are dropped.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ;
// '__arglist' is the variadic parameter of C#'s native-interop calling convention.
// There is no way to read the extra arguments in this model, so it is parsed and
// dropped: it pushes no name, and the method simply has one parameter fewer.
Param       = ArglistParam | { AttrSection } { ParamMod } Type [ "..." ] ParamName [ ParamDflt ] ;
ArglistParam = "__arglist" KwEnd ;
ParamName   <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
ParamDflt   <~~ push({pdflt: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" Expression ;
ParamMod    = ThisMod | ParamsMod | ( "ref" | "out" | "in" | "readonly" | "scoped" ) KwEnd ;
ThisMod     <~~ push({thisParam: true}) ~~>
            = "this" KwEnd ;
ParamsMod   <~~ push({paramsParam: true}) ~~>
            = "params" KwEnd ;

// Types are parsed and (apart from field defaults) ignored: generic arguments (<...>),
// dotted names, array suffixes and the nullable '?' are accepted and dropped.
// The pointer suffix ('int*', 'S**') is accepted and dropped: unsafe code parses, and a
// pointer behaves like the reference it names here.
// The nullable '?' may sit BEFORE the array suffix ('int?[]' is an array of nullable
// ints) or after it ('int[]?' is a nullable array); both are dropped. EXACTLY ONE of
// them may match, which is why they are two alternatives rather than two optionals:
// with both optional, `o is Program? ? 1 : 2` ate the ternary's '?' as a second
// nullable suffix and the conditional behind it no longer parsed.
Type        = TypeName ( "?" ArrSfxs | ArrSfxs [ "?" ] ) ;
ArrSfxs     = { "*" | "[" { "," } "]" } ;
TypeName    = TupleType | PrimType | "string" KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | "object" KwEnd | "void" KwEnd
            | "var" KwEnd | "dynamic" KwEnd | UserType ;
// A tuple type ('(int, int)', '(int lo, int hi)'). It needs at least two elements, so a
// parenthesized type is never read as one. The element names are part of the TYPE only;
// the value carries the names its literal spells out (see makeTuple).
TupleType   = "(" TupTyElem "," TupTyElem { "," TupTyElem } ")" ;
TupTyElem   = Type [ Id ] ;
PrimType    = ( "int" | "uint" | "long" | "ulong" | "short" | "ushort" | "byte" | "sbyte"
              | "double" | "float" | "decimal" | "bool" | "char" ) KwEnd ;
// 'A::B.C' - the namespace-alias qualifier of an 'extern alias' - reads like a dotted
// name; only the last segment is kept anywhere, so the alias itself never matters.
UserType    = Id [ "::" Id ] [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> nextBlock.
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = Block | If | While | Do | ForEach | For | Switch | Return | Break | Continue
            | Goto | Try | Throw | UsingStmt | Lock | Checked | FixedStmt | LocalFunc
            | YieldStmt | Decon | Decl | Empty | ExprStmt ;

// 'fixed (int* p = &v) stmt': pinning is not modelled (nothing here moves), so the
// statement is exactly its declaration followed by its body, scoped together - which
// is what a block of those two items already is. It precedes Decl because 'fixed' is
// also a LOCAL MODIFIER, and Decl would take the keyword and then fail on the '('.
FixedStmt   <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "fixed" KwEnd "(" DeclCore ")" Statement ;

// 'goto L;' is a branch to the basic block the enclosing statement list opened for L
// (label-as-basic-block, like batch-to-llvm-ir.abnf); 'goto case c;' and 'goto default;'
// re-enter the enclosing switch's dispatch instead - the scrutinee lives in a hidden
// scope variable, so 'goto case c' just rewrites it and branches back to the head. The
// longer forms are listed first, so 'goto default' is never read as a label named
// 'default'.
Goto        = GotoCase | GotoDflt | GotoLabel ;
GotoCase    <~~ push(makeGotoCase(pop())) ~~>
            = KwGoto KwCase CondExpr ";" ;
GotoDflt    <~~ push(makeGotoDefault()) ~~>
            = KwGoto KwDefault ";" ;
GotoLabel   <~~ push(makeGoto(pop())) ~~>
            = KwGoto LabelName ";" ;
LabelName   <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;

// A local function: a named closure over the enclosing locals, declared like a method.
// It is bound before its body can run, so it may call itself.
LocalFunc   <~~ push(makeLocalFunc(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] ParamList MethodBody ;

// 'checked { ... }' / 'unchecked { ... }': the block runs (overflow checking is not
// modelled); the keyword is dropped and the inner block thunk stands in for it.
Checked     = ( "checked" KwEnd | "unchecked" KwEnd | "unsafe" KwEnd ) Block ;

// 'lock (e) stmt': the monitor is not modelled; the guard expression still runs (so its
// calls stay visible) and the guarded statement executes.
Lock        <~~ push(makeLock(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = "lock" KwEnd "(" Expression ")" Statement ;

// 'using (init) stmt': the resource is created, the body runs, then Dispose() is called
// on it - and on several resources in reverse order. The 'using var r = e;' declaration
// form is a BlockItem rather than a Statement (it scopes the rest of its block, which is
// exactly the using statement C# rewrites it to - see makeBlockStmt).
UsingStmt   <~~ push(makeUsing(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwUsing "(" UsingInit ")" Statement ;
UsingInit   = UsingDecl | UsingExpr ;
// 'using (new Res())' is an expression, but 'new Res' also reads as a type plus a
// declarator - so a declaration here must not be followed by an argument list.
UsingDecl   <~~ push(makeUseDecl(pop())) ~~>
            = DeclCore !"(" ;
UsingExpr   <~~ push({useExpr: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;

// A block. 'using var r = e;' is a BlockItem of its own (not a Statement), so
// makeBlockStmt sees a marker record and can scope the rest of the block under it.
Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { BlockItem } "}" ;
BlockItem   = UsingItem | LabelItem | Statement ;
UsingItem   <~~ push({usingItem: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwUsing UsingDecl ";" ;
// A label ('done:'). It is a BlockItem of its own rather than a prefix of the statement
// it marks, so a label may also stand at the very end of a block. The '!":"' keeps the
// namespace alias qualifier 'A::B' out.
LabelItem   <~~ push({labelName: pop()}) ~~>
            = LabelName ":" !":" ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" Statement [ KwElse Statement ] ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" Statement ;

Do          <~~ push(makeDo(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwDo Statement KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ";" ;

// The colon switch (on int or string): starts at the first matching label and runs
// until a break or the end of the switch. Labels may stack.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" "{" { SwitchCase } "}" ;
SwitchCase  = Case | Default ;
// A case label is a PATTERN in C# 7 and later ('case int n when n > 0:', 'case > 5:',
// 'case string s:'). The constant form is tried FIRST, because an enum member
// ('case Color.Red:') reads as a type pattern otherwise and a PEG never goes back - and
// because only a constant label is a target for 'goto case c;'.
Case        = TypeKwCase | ConstCase | PatCase ;
// 'case string:' / 'case int n:'. A bare built-in type name can never begin a constant
// expression, so it has to be read as a type pattern before ConstCase gets to it - while
// 'case int.MaxValue:' still falls through, because the '.' defeats this rule.
TypeKwCase  <~~ push(makePatCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase TypeKwPat [ ArmGuard ] ":" { Statement } ;
TypeKwPat   <~~ push(csPatType(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TypeKw [ PatBind ] ;
TypeKw      <~~ push({patty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( PrimType | "string" KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | "object" KwEnd | "dynamic" KwEnd )
              { "*" | "[" { "," } "]" } [ "?" ] ;
ConstCase   <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase CondExpr ":" { Statement } ;
PatCase     <~~ push(makePatCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase Pattern [ ArmGuard ] ":" { Statement } ;
Default     <~~ push(makeDefault(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDefault ":" { Statement } ;

// foreach (var x in coll) body.
ForEach     <~~ push(makeForEach(pop(), pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwForeach "(" Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> KwIn Expression ")" Statement ;

For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" [ ForInit ] ";" [ ForCond ] ";" [ ForPost ] ")" Statement ;
ForInit     <~~ push({k:"init", t:pop()}) ~~> = ForVar | ForExpr ;
ForVar      = DeclCore ;
ForExpr     <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~> = Expression ;
ForCond     <~~ push({k:"cond", t:pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForPost     <~~ push({k:"post", t:exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression ] ";" ;

// An iterator block. A method whose body contains a 'yield' produces a SEQUENCE: the
// body runs once with a hidden accumulator, every 'yield return e' appends to it and
// 'yield break' ends it early, and the accumulator is what the call answers. The sequence
// is therefore materialized eagerly - an endless iterator does not terminate here - which
// is what makes 'foreach', LINQ-free enumeration and GetEnumerator/MoveNext/Current all
// work on the ordinary array representation.
YieldStmt   = YieldBreak | YieldReturn ;
YieldReturn <~~ push(makeYieldReturn(pop())) ~~>
            = KwYield KwReturn Expression ";" ;
YieldBreak  <~~ push(makeYieldBreak()) ~~>
            = KwYield KwBreak ";" ;
KwYield     = "yield" KwEnd ;

Break       <~~ push(makeBreak()) ~~>    = KwBreak ";" ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue()) ~~> = KwContinue ";" ;

// try/catch/finally and throw are implemented: throw calls js_throw, each try/catch/
// finally body compiles to its own closure handed to js_try, and a return/break/continue
// leaving a try or catch body becomes a control signal that excDispatch re-issues in the
// enclosing frame. The first catch clause wins (the exception type and an optional 'when
// (cond)' filter are parsed but not discriminated / evaluated). A catch may omit the parens
// ('catch { }'), the binding ('catch (T) { }') or bind a name ('catch (T e) { }').
Try         <~~ push(makeTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry TryBody { CatchClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;
CatchClause <~~ push(makeCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch [ "(" Type [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ")" ] [ KwWhen "(" DiscardExpr ")" ] Block ;
FinallyClause <~~ push({finbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwFinally Block ;
// throw e; raises e via js_throw; a bare throw; (rethrow) raises undefined (no value here).
Throw       <~~ push(makeThrow(csThrowVal(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = KwThrow [ Expression ] ";" ;

// A deconstruction statement: 'var (a, b) = e;' declares both names, '(a, b) = e;' assigns
// to names that already exist, and a typed element ('(int q, string s) = e') declares that
// one. The right side may be a tuple, or any object with a Deconstruct(out ...) method -
// see csDeconValue. Two elements at least, so '(x) = e' is never read as one.
Decon       <~~ push(makeDecon(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ DeconVar ] "(" DeconTgt "," DeconTgt { "," DeconTgt } ")" "=" Expression ";" ;
DeconVar    <~~ push({deconVar: true}) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd ;
DeconTgt    = DeconTyped | DeconPlain ;
DeconTyped  <~~ push({dtName: pop(), dtDecl: true}) ~~>
            = Type DeconId ;
DeconPlain  <~~ push({dtName: pop(), dtDecl: false}) ~~>
            = DeconId ;
DeconId     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;

// A local declaration. The declared type reaches the declarators (DType), so a variable
// without an initializer - and a bare '= default' - gets that type's default value.
Decl        = DeclCore ";" ;
DeclCore    <~~ push(makeDeclSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { LocalMod } DType Declarator { "," Declarator } ;
// Modifiers a LOCAL declaration may carry. They are parsed and dropped: 'const' and
// 'readonly' are compile-time promises, and 'ref' / 'scoped' locals alias rather than
// copy, which this model does not distinguish.
LocalMod    = ( "const" | "readonly" | "static" | "volatile" | "unsafe" | "ref"
              | "scoped" | "fixed" ) KwEnd ;
DType       <~~ push({dty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
Declarator  <~~ push(makeDeclInfo(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ DeclInit ] ;
DeclInit    = DeclDefault | DeclExpr ;
// '= default' without a type argument takes the declared type's default.
DeclDefault <~~ push({bareDefault: true}) ~~>
            = "=" "default" KwEnd !"(" ;
DeclExpr    <~~ push({dinit: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" InitVal ;

Empty       <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>
            = ";" ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ";" ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> {b, v}.
// A throw EXPRESSION ('n > 0 ? n : throw new Exception("neg")') raises instead of
// producing a value, so it may stand wherever a value is expected. Unlike the throw
// STATEMENT it does not terminate its block (the surrounding ternary still wants to
// branch to the merge); js_throw unwinds on the host side, so the code after it in
// the emitted block is unreachable at run time.
Expression  = RefExpr | Assignment | ThrowExpr | CondExpr ;
// `ref x` in expression position (a ref local's initializer, `return ref x`,
// `=> ref field`, `b ? ref x : ref y`). This model copies rather than aliases, so
// the `ref` is dropped and the operand stands for the whole thing - it carries no
// tag at all. `ref` is a reserved word, so nothing else can start this way, and an
// argument's own `ref` is taken by ArgRefMod before this is ever reached.
RefExpr     = "ref" KwEnd [ "readonly" KwEnd ] Expression ;
ThrowExpr   <~~ push(makeThrowExpr(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow CondExpr ;
// The compound bitwise/shift forms compile like their arithmetic siblings; '??=' is
// accepted but reported as not implemented (it needs a null test). Longer operators are
// listed before their prefixes.
Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Target ( "<<=" | ">>=" | "??=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%="
                     | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | ( "=" !"=" ) ) <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> Expression ;

// An assignable place: a variable (this and class names are variables too), or a
// member/index path: this.x, p.X, Program.Fails, arr[i], dict[k].
// `(a) = 1` wraps the target in parentheses, and the target's ROOT may be any
// primary at all - `((IF) ia).Prop = 3`, `GetList().Offset += added`,
// `t.GetMe().GetMe().bar++`, `(new Foo()).s += "A"`. ParenTarget carries no tag,
// so exactly one target record reaches the enclosing Assignment / IncDec.
Target      = ParenTarget | ChainTarget | PlainTarget ;
ParenTarget = "(" Target ")" ;
// The suffix run is one greedy repetition rather than "{ Sfx } LastSfx": a PEG
// repetition never gives a match back, so the two-part spelling could never see
// its final suffix. The LAST suffix the run consumed is the place. PlaceAhead is
// a fast-fail: without it every expression is parsed twice per nesting level.
ChainTarget <~~ push(makeChainTarget(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PlaceAhead CallSeed ChainSfx { ChainSfx } ;
ChainSfx    = MethodSfx | CallSfx | FieldSfx | IndexSfx ;
PlaceAhead  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0 // Whitespace is not skipped before a :script, so scan past it first.
        while (c.peek(i) === 32 || c.peek(i) === 9 || c.peek(i) === 10 || c.peek(i) === 13) { i++ }
        var depth = 0
        var n = 0
        var found = false
        var done = false
        while (!done && n < 4000) {
            var ch = c.peek(i)
            if (ch < 0) { done = true }
            else if (depth > 0) {
                if (ch === 34 || ch === 39) {
                    var q0 = ch
                    i++
                    var g1 = 0
                    while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== q0 && g1 < 4000) {
                        if (c.peek(i) === 92) { i++ }
                        i++
                        g1++
                    }
                    i++
                }
                else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                    while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                }
                else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                    i = i + 2
                    var g2 = 0
                    while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && g2 < 40000) {
                        if (c.peek(i) === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { g2 = 40000 } else { i++ }
                        g2++
                    }
                    i = i + 2
                }
                else if (ch === 40 || ch === 91 || ch === 123) { depth++; i++ }
                else if (ch === 41 || ch === 93 || ch === 125) { depth--; i++ }
                else { i++ }
            }
            else if (ch === 47 && (c.peek(i + 1) === 47 || c.peek(i + 1) === 42)) {
                if (c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                    while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                } else {
                    i = i + 2
                    var g3 = 0
                    while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && g3 < 40000) {
                        if (c.peek(i) === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { g3 = 40000 } else { i++ }
                        g3++
                    }
                    i = i + 2
                }
            }
            else if (ch === 40 || ch === 91) { depth++; i++ }
            // At depth 0 only a MEMBER PATH may stand before the operator, so a
            // name character, a dot, a `->` and whitespace continue the scan and
            // anything else settles it. That bound keeps the scan proportional to
            // the target rather than to the whole statement.
            else if (ch === 46 || ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 36 || ch === 95
                     || (ch >= 48 && ch <= 57) || (ch >= 65 && ch <= 90) || (ch >= 97 && ch <= 122) || ch >= 128) { i++ }
            else if (ch === 61) { found = c.peek(i + 1) !== 61; done = true }
            else if (ch === 45 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { i = i + 2 }
            else if (ch === 43 || ch === 45) {
                found = c.peek(i + 1) === ch || c.peek(i + 1) === 61
                done = true
            }
            else if (ch === 42 || ch === 47 || ch === 37 || ch === 38 || ch === 124 || ch === 94) {
                found = c.peek(i + 1) === 61
                done = true
            }
            else if (ch === 63 && c.peek(i + 1) === 63 && c.peek(i + 2) === 61) { found = true; done = true }
            else if (ch === 60 || ch === 62) {
                var q = i
                var run = 0
                while (c.peek(q) === ch) { q++; run++ }
                found = run >= 2 && c.peek(q) === 61
                done = true
            }
            else { done = true }
            n++
        }
        return found ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01no-place", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
PlainTarget <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;

TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression { "," DiscardExpr } "]" ;

CondExpr    <~~ push(makeCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NullCoal [ "?" Expression ":" Expression ] ;

// Null-coalescing 'a ?? b': the first operand that is not null wins.
NullCoal    <~~ push(makeNullCoal(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = OrExpr { "??" ( ThrowExpr | OrExpr ) } ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "||" AndExpr } ;

AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = BitOr { "&&" BitOr } ;

// The bitwise levels sit between && and equality (as in C#). The !"|" / !"&"
// lookaheads make each level refuse the FIRST character of the doubled operator. This
// used to be left to the right side failing and the iteration rolling back - which
// worked only while nothing could start with '&': once AddrOf ('&x') existed,
// `false && Bump()` parsed as `false & (&Bump())`, evaluated bitwise, and stopped
// short-circuiting. Silently: it is a wrong ANSWER, not a parse error, and only the
// logic-and-skipped assertion in csharp-test-features.cs caught it.
BitOr       <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitXor { "|" !"|" <~~ push("|") ~~> BitXor } ;
BitXor      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitAnd { "^" <~~ push("^") ~~> BitAnd } ;
BitAnd      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = EqExpr { "&" !"&" <~~ push("&") ~~> EqExpr } ;

EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "==" | "!=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;

RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TypeTest { ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> TypeTest } ;

// 'e is Pattern' / 'e as Type'. The patterns modelled are the constant ones (null, true,
// false) and the type pattern, with an optional binding ('o is string s') and an optional
// 'not'. 'e as T' yields e when it is a T and null otherwise. csIsType is the runtime type
// test; a binding declares its name in the enclosing scope, as C# does.
TypeTest    <~~ push(makeTypeTest(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Shift [ IsAs | SwitchExpr ] ;
IsAs        = AsTest | IsTest ;
AsTest      <~~ push({asty: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwAs TypeArg ;
IsTest      <~~ push({ispat: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwIs Pattern ;

// The switch EXPRESSION ('n switch { > 8 => "A", _ => "C" }'): the first arm whose
// pattern matches - and whose optional 'when' guard holds - supplies the value. It is a
// suffix of the shift level, so 'e switch {...} == x' still compares the result.
SwitchExpr  <~~ push({swarms: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "{" [ SwitchArm { "," SwitchArm } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
SwitchArm   <~~ push(csSwitchArm(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Pattern [ ArmGuard ] "=>" Expression ;
ArmGuard    <~~ push({guard: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwWhen Expression ;

// ----- patterns -----
// The C# pattern grammar: constant, relational, type / declaration, var, discard,
// property, positional and list patterns, combined with 'and' / 'or' / 'not'. Every
// production pushes exactly one pattern record; the matcher walks that record.
Pattern     = OrPat ;
OrPat       <~~ push(csPatOr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AndPat { KwOr AndPat } ;
AndPat      <~~ push(csPatAnd(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NotPat { KwAnd NotPat } ;
NotPat      = NotForm | PrimPat ;
NotForm     <~~ push(csPatNot(pop())) ~~>
            = KwNot NotPat ;
// Ordered longest-first: a property pattern and a positional pattern both BEGIN with a
// type name, so the bare type pattern has to be tried after both of them - a PEG never
// goes back. The constant pattern is last, because a type name reads as an expression too.
PrimPat     = RelPat | NullPat | TruePat | FalsePat | DiscardPat | VarPat | ListPat
            | PosPatT | PropPat | PosPat | ParenPat | TypePat | ConstPat ;
RelPat      <~~ push(csPatRel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> Shift ;
NullPat     <~~ push(csPatNull()) ~~>
            = "null" KwEnd ;
TruePat     <~~ push(csPatConst(makeConst(true))) ~~>
            = "true" KwEnd ;
FalsePat    <~~ push(csPatConst(makeConst(false))) ~~>
            = "false" KwEnd ;
DiscardPat  <~~ push(csPatDiscard()) ~~>
            = "_" KwEnd ;
VarPat      <~~ push(csPatVar(pop())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd PatName ;
PatName     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
// A list pattern ('[1, _, 3]', '[1, ..]', '[.. var rest]'): a '..' element matches any
// run of elements, and may itself carry a pattern for the slice it covers.
ListPat     <~~ push(csPatList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ ListElem { "," ListElem } ] [ "," ] "]" [ PatBind ] ;
ListElem    = SlicePat | Pattern ;
SlicePat    <~~ push(csPatSlice(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ".." [ Pattern ] ;
PosPatT     <~~ push(csPatPos(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatType "(" Pattern { "," Pattern } ")" [ PatBind ] ;
PropPat     <~~ push(csPatProp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ PatType ] "{" [ PropSub { "," PropSub } ] [ "," ] "}" [ PatBind ] ;
PropSub     <~~ push(csPatSub(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatName ":" !":" Pattern ;
PosPat      <~~ push(csPatPos(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" Pattern "," Pattern { "," Pattern } ")" [ PatBind ] ;
ParenPat    = "(" Pattern ")" ;
TypePat     <~~ push(csPatType(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatType [ PatBind ] ;
ConstPat    <~~ push(csPatConst(pop())) ~~>
            = Shift ;
PatType     <~~ push({patty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
PatBind     <~~ push({patbind: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;
KwAnd       = "and" KwEnd ;
KwOr        = "or" KwEnd ;
KwNot       = "not" KwEnd ;

Shift       <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ( "<<" | ">>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AddExpr } ;

AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;

MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr } ;

// 'await e': the async machinery is not modelled (an async member runs synchronously),
// so await is the identity on its operand - which is what a synchronously completed task
// means. It is a rule without a tag, so the operand's own thunk stands.
// PreIncDec comes before Neg, or '--x' reads as the double negation '-(-x)'.
// '&x' (address-of) and '*p' (dereference) are identity here: a pointer behaves like
// the reference it names, so both operators pass their operand through untagged.
// Neither shadows the BINARY '&' or '*', which are only reached after an operand has
// already been parsed - but see the !"&" on the bitwise level above, which is what
// keeps `a && b` from being read as `a & (&b)`.
UnaryExpr   = Await | PreIncDec | Cast | Neg | UPlus | Not | BitNot | AddrOf | Deref | PostfixExpr ;
// Unary + is the identity, so it needs no tag. The `!"+"` keeps it away from the
// `++` that PreIncDec above already owns.
UPlus       = "+" !"+" UnaryExpr ;
AddrOf      = "&" !"=" UnaryExpr ;
Deref       = "*" !"=" UnaryExpr ;
PreIncDec   <~~ push(makePreIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Target ;
Await       = "await" KwEnd UnaryExpr ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" UnaryExpr ;
BitNot      <~~ push(makeBitNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "~" UnaryExpr ;

// A cast is lowered as identity ((Type) expr yields expr). A primitive cast accepts any
// unary operand; a reference cast excludes a leading '-'/'+' so '(a) - b' stays subtraction.
Cast        = PrimCast | RefCast ;
PrimCast    <~~ push(makeCast(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = "(" CastPrim { "*" | "[" "]" } [ "?" ] ")" UnaryExpr ;
CastPrim    <~~ push({castty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = PrimType ;
RefCast     <~~ push(makeCast(pop())) ~~>
            = "(" RefType ")" UnaryNoPM ;
// Cast is in here so a cast of a cast - `(TService) (object) x`, which the mono
// suite is full of - parses; without it the inner cast is not a legal operand of
// the outer one.
UnaryNoPM   = Not | Cast | PostfixExpr ;
// The trailing '?' is the NULLABLE suffix of a cast target - '(MyStruct?) s'. It is
// inside the parentheses, so the ternary '(cond) ? a : b' is untouched.
RefType     = ( "string" KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | UserType ) { "*" | "[" "]" } [ "?" ] ;

// 'new C().m()' and 'base.M().n()' continue into the member chain, so an object
// creation and a base call are SEEDS of the chain (CallSeed), not alternatives beside it.
PostfixExpr = IncDec | CallMember ;
IncDec      <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// new Point(1, 2) | new List<int>() | new Dictionary<K,V>() | new int[]{...} | new int[n]
// | new Foo{ X = 1 } (object initializer) | new Foo(a){ X = 1 } (ctor + initializer)
NewExpr     = NewAnon | NewImplArr | NewObjInit | NewArrLit | NewArrSize | NewObject | StackAlloc ;
// An anonymous type ('new { X = 1, Y = 2 }'): a plain object with those members.
NewAnon     <~~ push(makeAnon(pop())) ~~>
            = KwNew AnonInit ;
// An anonymous type has its OWN initializer list, because it alone may PROJECT a
// member instead of naming one: `new { a.B, C }` is `new { B = a.B, C = C }`
// (C# 7.6.10.6). Putting the projection in the shared ObjInit instead made a
// collection initializer - `new List<int> { 1, 2 }` - match it, and every entry
// became a member named after its own text.
AnonInit    <~~ push({objinit: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" [ AnonMember { "," AnonMember } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
AnonMember  = NestedInit | IndexedInit | PlainInit | ProjInit ;
ProjInit    <~~ push(makeMemberInit(pop(), csProjName(up.in))) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// An object initializer without constructor parens: 'new Foo{ X = 1, Y = 2 }' runs the
// parameterless constructor, then assigns each named member. Tried before NewArrLit so a
// collection literal ('new List<int>{1, 2}', whose entries are plain expressions) still
// falls through to it.
NewObjInit  <~~ push(makeNew(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewName ObjInit ;
// The created type's name. It may be qualified ('new Outer.Inner()', 'new System.Text.X()');
// only the last segment is kept, which is the simple name every type is registered under.
// The generic arguments are kept too (reified type arguments, see makeNew).
// The trailing '?' is the NULLABLE suffix - `new S? ()` creates a Nullable<S>,
// which this model does not distinguish from an S. csSimpleName drops it.
NewName     <~~ push({cname: csSimpleName(up.in), targs: csTypeArgs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id [ "::" Id ] [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } [ "?" ] ;
// The array type suffix ('[]') is part of Type, so 'new int[]{...}' consumes it here.
NewArrLit   <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew Type "{" [ ArrElem { "," ArrElem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
// 'new[] { 1, 2, 3 }': an implicitly typed array - no type name at all.
NewImplArr  <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew "[" { "," } "]" "{" [ ArrElem { "," ArrElem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
// 'new A[1] { new A() }': a sized array MAY still carry an initializer, which wins.
NewArrSize  <~~ push(makeSizedArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew Type "[" ArrDim { "," ArrDim } "]" { "[" { "," } "]" } [ ArrInit ] ;
// `stackalloc T[n]` / `stackalloc T[] { ... }`: a stack buffer. Nothing here has a
// stack, so it allocates the same array `new T[n]` would - which is all a program
// that only reads and writes it can observe.
StackAlloc  = StackSized | StackLit ;
StackSized  <~~ push(makeSizedArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwStackalloc Type "[" ArrDim { "," ArrDim } "]" [ ArrInit ] ;
StackLit    <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwStackalloc Type "{" [ ArrElem { "," ArrElem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
ArrDim      <~~ push({adim: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
ArrInit     <~~ push({ainit: pop()}) ~~>
            = ArrLit ;
NewObject   <~~ push(makeNew(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewName "(" ArgList ")" [ ObjInit | SkipBlock ] ;
// { X = expr, ... }: the trailing member initializer of an object creation.
ObjInit     <~~ push({objinit: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" [ MemberInit { "," MemberInit } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
// 'X = e' assigns the member; 'P1 = { X = 1 }' is a NESTED initializer, which assigns
// into the object the member already holds rather than replacing it; '[k] = e' is an
// indexed initializer.
MemberInit  = NestedInit | IndexedInit | PlainInit ;
PlainInit   <~~ push(makeMemberInit(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" Expression ;
NestedInit  <~~ push(makeNestedInit(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" ObjInit ;
IndexedInit <~~ push(makeIndexedInit(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" "=" Expression ;

// base.M(args): the method search starts at the base class of the class the call
// appears in (curClassName is set while the class body is being built).
BaseCall    <~~ push(makeBaseCall(popName(), takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwBase "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" ArgList ")" ;

// A primary followed by method calls, delegate calls, field reads and indexing. A bare
// '(args)' suffix invokes a delegate / lambda value (a closure call).
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CallSeed { MethodSfx | CallSfx | FieldSfx | IndexSfx
                      | NCMethodSfx | NCFieldSfx | NCIndexSfx | NFSfx | WithSfx } ;
// 'r with { Y = 9 }': a copy of the record with those members replaced.
WithSfx     <~~ push({kind: "with", winits: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwWith ObjInit ;
KwWith      = "with" KwEnd ;
// The null-forgiving operator 'e!': a compile-time assertion in C#, identity here. The
// '=' lookahead keeps 'a != b' out.
NFSfx       <~~ push({kind: "nfg"}) ~~>
            = "!" !"=" ;
// The head of a member chain. A bare 'M(args)' call inside a class body means
// 'this.M(args)' (or the class' own static M) when the enclosing class declares an M;
// otherwise it is an ordinary call of whatever the name holds (a lambda, a delegate).
// typeof / nameof / sizeof / default look exactly like a bare call, so they are matched
// before SelfCall.
// Lambda and AnonMethod precede SelfCall, which is a bare `Id(args)` call: without
// that order `delegate () { }` and `async () => e` are read as calls to variables
// NAMED `delegate` and `async` (both match Id, and both are followed by `()`), the
// call succeeds, and the block or the `=>` behind it is then left unparsed. Neither
// new alternative can shadow anything: one demands the `delegate` keyword, the other
// an `=>` after its head.
CallSeed    = KwExpr | NewExpr | BaseCall | Lambda | AnonMethod
            | AliasSeed | GenSeed | SelfCall | Primary | VarSeed ;
// 'G<int>.Instance' / 'C<object>.Foo(x)' - a CONSTRUCTED type name as the receiver of
// a static member. Type arguments are erased, so the seed is the plain class name and
// the member behind it is an ordinary field or call suffix; GenSeed pushes both, which
// is all foldCallMember needs.
//
// The '.' is consumed HERE rather than left to FieldSfx on purpose: a seed of
// 'Id SkipAngle' alone would read the relational chain 'a < b > c' as a constructed
// type, and a PEG never goes back. Requiring the '.' costs nothing real - a
// constructed type is only ever a receiver - and keeps every comparison intact.
GenSeed     = GenName "." GenMemb ;
// The type may itself be NESTED - `A<int>.B<ulong>.foo()`, `Outer.Inner<string>.V`,
// `HasherBuilder.ByPrototype<U>.Examine()`. Every alternative still demands at
// least one `<...>`, which together with the `"." GenMemb` that follows is what
// keeps the relational chain `a < b > c` from being read as a constructed type.
// csSimpleName drops the type arguments and keeps the last segment, which is the
// simple name every nested type is registered under.
GenName     <~~ push(csGenSeed(csSimpleName(up.in))) ~~>
            = Id SkipAngle { "." Id SkipAngle DotAhead }
            | Id "." Id SkipAngle { "." Id SkipAngle DotAhead }
            | Id "." Id "." Id SkipAngle { "." Id SkipAngle DotAhead } ;
// A further `.Name<args>` segment is only part of the TYPE when another `.`
// follows it; otherwise it is the generic METHOD being called, and GenMemb has to
// see it. The repetition cannot give a match back, so the test belongs inside the
// iteration: `C<object>.foo<int>(null)` would otherwise lose its member.
DotAhead    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = 0 // Whitespace is not skipped before a :script, so scan past it first.
        while (c.peek(s) === 32 || c.peek(s) === 9 || c.peek(s) === 10 || c.peek(s) === 13) { s++ }
        return c.peek(s) === 46 ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01no-dot", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

GenMemb     = GenMCall | GenField ;
GenMCall    <~~ push({kind:"mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" ArgList ")" ;
GenField    = Id <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
// 'MyAssembly01::GlobalClass' - the namespace-alias qualifier an 'extern alias'
// enables. There are no assemblies here, so the alias is dropped and the name behind
// it seeds the chain exactly as a bare name would. It has to beat SelfCall and
// VarSeed, which would both read the ALIAS as the name and stop at the '::'.
AliasSeed   = Id "::" AliasTail ;
AliasTail   = AliasCall | AliasVar ;
AliasCall   <~~ push(makeSelfCall(popName(), takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" ArgList ")" ;
AliasVar    <~~ push({seedvar: up.in, seedt: makeVarRef(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id ;
// A bare name in SEED position. It pushes its own name beside its thunk, so the member
// chain above it knows which variable it started at - which is what a call of a HIDDEN
// method ('public new string Tag()') needs, since C# resolves that one by the DECLARED
// type of the receiver rather than by the object's own class. Listed after Primary so
// 'true' / 'false' / 'null' are still keywords rather than identifiers.
VarSeed     <~~ push({seedvar: up.in, seedt: makeVarRef(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id ;
KwExpr      = TypeofExpr | NameofExpr | SizeofExpr | DefaultExpr ;
SelfCall    <~~ push(makeSelfCall(popName(), takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" ArgList ")" ;

MethodSfx   <~~ push({kind:"mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" ArgList ")" ;
CallSfx     <~~ push({kind:"call", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" ArgList ")" ;
FieldSfx    = "." Id <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;

// A call's argument list. A NAMED argument ('S22Vol(1, d: 5)') keeps its parameter name
// beside its thunk; csFixArgs turns the mixed list back into a positional one using the
// parameter names the declaration registered (csParamNames), and fills the skipped slots
// with the 'omitted' marker (undefined) that the parameter binder answers with the
// parameter's default. 'ref' / 'out' / 'in' at a call site are parsed and dropped.
ArgList     = [ Arg { "," Arg } ] ;
Arg         = OutVarArg | NamedArg | PlainArg ;
// `M(out int x)` / `M(out var x)` (C# 7): the argument DECLARES the variable, in
// the enclosing scope. There are no genuine out parameters in this model, so the
// declaration is what is kept: the name is bound to null before the call, which
// is what makes the uses after the call resolve.
OutVarArg   <~~ push(csOutVar(pop())) ~~>
            = ( "out" | "ref" ) KwEnd Type OutVarName ;
OutVarName  <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
NamedArg    <~~ push(csNamedArg(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ArgName ":" !":" [ ArgRefMod ] Expression ;
ArgName     <~~ push({argname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;
PlainArg    = [ ArgRefMod ] Expression ;
ArgRefMod   = ( "ref" | "out" | "in" ) KwEnd ;

// A multi-dimensional index ('b[0, 0]') is accepted; the model has only flat arrays,
// so the further subscripts are evaluated (their calls stay visible) and dropped.
IndexSfx    <~~ push(csIndexSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" IdxArg { "," IdxArg } "]" ;
// An index argument: a plain index, an index counted from the END ('^1'), or a range
// ('1..3', '1..', '..3', '..'), which slices. It is written as one optional index
// followed by an optional '..' tail, so an ordinary index is still parsed exactly once
// (a 'range | index' choice would parse every index twice, which nests exponentially).
// An indexer takes NAMED arguments too (`b["a", arg2: 20]`). The model indexes
// positionally, so the name is parsed and dropped; it has to be tried before the
// ordinary index, which would read the name as an expression and die on the ':'.
IdxArg      = NamedIdx | ( [ IdxOne ] [ IdxTail ] ) ;
NamedIdx    = ArgName2 ":" !":" Expression ;
ArgName2    = Id ;
IdxOne      = FromEnd | Expression ;
FromEnd     <~~ push({fromEnd: pop()}) ~~>
            = "^" Expression ;
IdxTail     <~~ push(csIdxTail(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ".." [ IdxOne ] ;

// Null-conditional access ('a?.b', 'a?.m(...)', 'a?[i]'): when the receiver is null the
// WHOLE remaining chain is skipped and the value is null. The '?' must be followed by '.'
// or '[' so the ternary '?' is never captured here.
NCMethodSfx <~~ push({kind: "mcall", nullsafe: true, name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "?" "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" ArgList ")" ;
NCFieldSfx  = "?" "." Id <~~ push({kind: "field", nullsafe: true, name: up.in}) ~~> ;
NCIndexSfx  <~~ push({kind: "index", nullsafe: true, idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "?" "[" Expression "]" ;

// A collection expression ('int[] xs = [1, 2, 3];', 'List<int> ys = [10, .. xs, 20];').
// Arrays and List<T> share one representation here, so both spell out the same value; a
// '..' spread element splices the elements of its operand in place.
CollExpr    <~~ push(makeCollection(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ CollElem { "," CollElem } ] [ "," ] "]" ;
CollElem    = SpreadElem | Expression ;
SpreadElem  <~~ push({spread: pop()}) ~~>
            = ".." Expression ;

Primary     = CollExpr | Lambda | AnonMethod | LinqQuery | "(" Expression ")" | TupleExpr
            | RawStr | VerbatimStr | InterpStr | DqString | CharLit
            | CsNum | IntLit | True | False | Null ;

// A tuple literal ('(1, "one")', '(lo: 3, hi: 9)'). It is listed AFTER the parenthesized
// expression, so an ordinary '(e)' is parsed exactly once: only a form that carries a
// top-level comma reaches this rule at all.
TupleExpr   <~~ push(makeTuple(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" TupItem "," TupItem { "," TupItem } ")" ;
TupItem     = TupNamed | TupPlain ;
TupNamed    <~~ push(csTupItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TupName ":" !":" Expression ;
TupName     <~~ push({tupname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;
TupPlain    <~~ push(csTupItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// typeof(T) yields a small type token (a .Name / .FullName carrying object, never null);
// nameof(x) is the last segment of its argument, resolved at parse time; sizeof(T) is the
// size of a primitive; default(T) is T's default value (0 / false / the null char / null,
// and a zeroed instance for a struct). A bare 'default' outside a declaration is null.
TypeofExpr  <~~ push(makeTypeof(pop())) ~~>
            = "typeof" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
NameofExpr  <~~ push(makeNameof(pop())) ~~>
            = "nameof" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
SizeofExpr  <~~ push(makeSizeof(pop())) ~~>
            = "sizeof" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
DefaultExpr = DefaultOf | DefaultBare ;
DefaultOf   <~~ push(makeDefaultOf(pop())) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
DefaultBare <~~ push(makeDefaultOf("")) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd ;
TypeArg     <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = Type ;

// A char literal 'x' (escapes included). C#'s char is an integral type that RENDERS
// as its glyph, so the value is a real char (a boxed code): it does arithmetic and
// compares as its code, and prints as the character. Reuses the balanced SkipChar shape.
CharLit     <~~ push(makeCharLit(up.in)) ~~> = SkipChar ;

// A raw string literal (triple-quoted): no escapes at all, and one or two quotes may
// appear inside it. Listed before the plain string so the opening triple quote is never
// read as the empty string followed by junk. A leading newline (the multi-line form) is stripped together
// with the indentation the closing delimiter sits at - see csRaw.
RawStr      <~~ push(makeConst(csRaw(up.in))) ~~>
            = '"' '"' '"' :whitespace() { RawQ2 | RawQ1 | RawPlain } '"' '"' '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
RawQ2       = '"' '"' RawPlain ;
RawQ1       = '"' RawPlain ;
RawPlain    = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// Verbatim strings. '@"..."' takes every byte literally (including newlines and
// backslashes); '""' is the escaped quote. '$@"..."' / '@$"..."' is the same text with
// '{expr}' interpolation holes, so it shares the interpolation productions.
VerbatimStr = VerbInterp | VerbPlain ;
VerbInterp  <~~ push(makeTemplate(takeAll())) ~~>
            = VerbIntro '"' :whitespace() { IsBrace | IsExpr | VbChunk } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
VerbIntro   = "$@" | "@$" ;
VbChunk     <~~ push({s: csVbText(up.in)}) ~~>
            = VbTxCh { VbTxCh } ;
VbTxCh      = VbDQuote | VbPlain ;
VbPlain     = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
VerbPlain   <~~ push(makeConst(csVerb(up.in))) ~~>
            = "@" '"' :whitespace() { VbDQuote | VbChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
VbDQuote    = '"' '"' ;
VbChar      = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// C# numeric literals. The value is computed by csNum, which is also where the
// literal's TYPE is decided (ECMA-334 6.4.5.3). CsDecP - the plain decimal
// integer - is listed LAST but is no longer left to the shared IntLit, because
// IntLit reads it with parseInt, i.e. through a double: 18446744073709551615
// came back as 18446744073709552000, and the two script engines even rounded it
// differently. CsNum precedes IntLit in the literal choice, so IntLit is now
// unreachable for this grammar.
CsNum       <~~ push(makeNumConst(csNum(up.in))) ~~>
            = CsHex | CsBin | CsDot | CsReal | CsDecU | CsDecS | CsDecP ;
CsHex       = ( "0x" | "0X" ) :whitespace() CsHexD { CsHexD | "_" } [ CsISfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsBin       = ( "0b" | "0B" ) :whitespace() CsBit { CsBit | "_" } [ CsISfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A leading-dot real ('.5'): only a digit may follow the dot, so a member access
// ('a.b') is never taken for a number.
CsDot       = "." :whitespace() "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ CsExp ] [ CsRSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsReal      = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" | "_" } CsRealT :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsDecU      = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } "_" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ CsISfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsDecS      = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } CsISfx :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsDecP      = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsRealT     = "." "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ CsExp ] [ CsRSfx ] | CsExp [ CsRSfx ] | CsRSfx ;
CsExp       = ( "e" | "E" ) [ "+" | "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
CsHexD      = "0"..."9" | "a"..."f" | "A"..."F" ;
CsBit       = "0" | "1" ;
CsISfx      = ( "u" | "U" | "l" | "L" ) [ "u" | "U" | "l" | "L" ] ;
CsRSfx      = "f" | "F" | "d" | "D" | "m" | "M" ;

// A lambda ('x => e', '(a, b) => { ... }') is a real closure value (a Func / Action
// delegate): it captures the current scope chain and is invoked at a call site (CallSfx).
Lambda      <~~ push(makeLambda(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LambdaMod ] LambdaHead "=>" LambdaBody ;
// 'static x => ...' / 'async x => ...': parsed and dropped (capture is not restricted).
LambdaMod   = ( "static" | "async" ) KwEnd ;
LambdaHead  = LamParenHead | LamBareHead ;
LamParenHead <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ LamParam { "," LamParam } ] ")" ;
LamBareHead <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = LamBareParam ;
// A lambda parameter is a full parameter (type, 'params', a default value) or a bare
// name. The full form is an ordered alternative so a bare name is not swallowed by a
// greedy optional type.
LamParam    = Param | LamBareParam ;
LamBareParam <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
// The anonymous-method form 'delegate (int x) { ... }': a lambda with a block body.
// The parameter list may be omitted entirely ('delegate { ... }'), which in C# means
// 'whatever this delegate type takes, ignored' - here an empty parameter list.
AnonMethod  = AnonParams | AnonBare ;
AnonParams  <~~ push(makeLambda(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDelegate ParamList LamBlock ;
AnonBare    <~~ push(makeBareAnon(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDelegate LamBlock ;
LambdaBody  = LamBlock | LamExpr ;
LamBlock    <~~ push({body: pop()}) ~~> = Block ;
LamExpr     <~~ push({ebody: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
// A LINQ query expression: accepted, reported as not implemented.
// The whole query-expression syntax (C# 7.16) is RECOGNIZED - from/let/where/
// join/orderby/group/select and the `into` continuation - and then reported as
// not implemented, like it was before: every sub-expression goes through
// DiscardExpr, so nothing is left on the stack.
//
// The range variable's explicit TYPE (`from int i in a`) is a separate
// alternative rather than an optional `[ Type ]`: a PEG optional that matched
// would never be given back, and `from i in a` has Type swallow `i`.
LinqQuery   <~~ push(makeLinq(up.pos)) ~~>
            = LinqFrom LinqBody ;
LinqFrom    = KwFrom Type Id KwIn DiscardExpr | KwFrom Id KwIn DiscardExpr ;
LinqBody    = { LinqClause } LinqResult [ LinqCont ] ;
// `... into g <more clauses>` restarts the body with a new range variable.
LinqCont    = KwInto Id LinqBody ;
LinqResult  = KwSelect DiscardExpr | KwGroup DiscardExpr KwBy DiscardExpr ;
LinqClause  = LinqFrom
            | KwWhere DiscardExpr
            | KwOrderby LinqOrd { "," LinqOrd }
            | KwLet Id "=" DiscardExpr
            | LinqJoin ;
LinqJoin    = KwJoin Type Id KwIn DiscardExpr KwOn DiscardExpr KwEquals DiscardExpr [ KwInto Id ]
            | KwJoin Id KwIn DiscardExpr KwOn DiscardExpr KwEquals DiscardExpr [ KwInto Id ] ;
LinqOrd     = DiscardExpr [ ( "ascending" | "descending" ) KwEnd ] ;

DiscardExpr <~~ pop() ~~>
            = Expression ;

Null        <~~ push(makeConst(null)) ~~>  = "null" KwEnd ;


// $"...{expr}..." interpolated string; a plain "..." (DqString) does not interpolate.
// A hole may carry an alignment ('{x,4}' pads to width 4, a negative width pads on the
// right) and a format specifier ('{x:D3}' is the decimal form zero-padded to 3 digits).
// '{{' and '}}' are the escaped braces. IsBrace comes first so '{{' is never read as the
// start of a hole.
InterpStr   <~~ push(makeTemplate(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "$" '"' :whitespace() { IsBrace | IsExpr | IsChunk } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
IsBrace     <~~ push({s: up.in.substring(0, 1)}) ~~>
            = "{" "{" | "}" "}" ;
IsExpr      <~~ push(makeIsExpr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace(Whitespace) Expression [ IsAlign ] [ IsFmt ] :whitespace() "}" ;
IsAlign     <~~ push({align: parseInt(stripWs(up.in).substring(1))}) ~~>
            = "," :whitespace() IsAlignN :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
IsAlignN    = "-" IsDigit { IsDigit } | IsDigit { IsDigit } ;
IsDigit     = "0"..."9" ;
IsFmt       <~~ push({fmt: up.in.substring(1)}) ~~>
            = ":" :whitespace() IsFmtCh { IsFmtCh } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
IsFmtCh     = "\x20"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
IsChunk     <~~ push({s: unescapeJs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = IsChar { IsChar } ;
IsChar      = IsEsc | IsPlain ;
IsEsc       = "\\" "\x20"..b"\x7e" ;
IsPlain     = "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" | @"\t" ;

// ----- Widened surface: generics, casts, unsupported declarations (see :description) -----

// Balanced skips let a construct be ACCEPTED without being understood. String and char
// literals are matched first so braces/brackets inside them are never miscounted.
SkipBlock   = "{" { SkipItem } "}" ;
SkipItem    = SkipStr | SkipChar | SkipBlock | SkipOther ;
SkipStr     = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipStrChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipStrChar = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChar    = "'" :whitespace() { SkipChEsc | SkipChChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipChEsc   = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced < ... > run (generic parameters / arguments), parsed and ignored.
SkipAngle   = "<" { SkipAngle | SkipAngleItem } ">" ;
SkipAngleItem = "\x00"..."\x3b" | "\x3d" | "\x3f"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced ( ... ) run (constructor init arguments), parsed and ignored.
SkipParen   = "(" { SkipStr | SkipChar | SkipParen | SkipPOther } ")" ;
SkipPOther  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x2a"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced [ ... ] run: an attribute section (also '[assembly: ...]'), parsed and
// ignored. Strings and chars are matched first so brackets inside them are not miscounted.
AttrSection = "[" { SkipStr | SkipChar | AttrSection | AttrOther } "]" ;
AttrOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5a" | "\x5c" | "\x5e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A generic constraint clause ('where T : class, IFoo, new()'): accepted and ignored.
WhereClause = "where" KwEnd Id ":" WhereCon { "," WhereCon } ;
WhereCon    = "new" KwEnd "(" ")" | Type ;

KwIs        = "is" KwEnd ;
KwAs        = "as" KwEnd ;

// 'new' is the method-hiding modifier ('public new string Tag()'). It only reaches here
// in member position, so the 'new' of an object creation is never taken for a modifier.
ModKw       = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "internal" | "static" | "abstract"
              | "sealed" | "virtual" | "override" | "readonly" | "const" | "partial"
              | "unsafe" | "async" | "extern" | "volatile" | "new" | "required" | "event"
              | "file" | "fixed" | "ref" | "scoped" ) KwEnd ;
LeadMods    = { ModKw | AttrSection } ;

// Type declarations accepted structurally, then reported as not implemented (so a whole
// C# file compiles; -warn-unsupported lets the rest run). Bodies are skipped.
UnsupType   = DelegateDecl ;
// An enum: a descriptor object of int constants. A member without a value continues
// from the previous one (starting at 0), and a member's initializer may name the members
// before it ('AB = A | B'), so the constants are declared in a scope of their own while
// the bodies are emitted.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods KwEnum EnumName [ ":" Type ] "{" [ EnumMem { "," EnumMem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
EnumName    <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
EnumMem     <~~ push(makeEnumMem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { AttrSection } Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ EnumInit ] ;
EnumInit    <~~ push({einit: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" Expression ;
// A delegate type declaration ('delegate int Op(int x);'). A delegate value is just the
// closure it names here, so the declaration itself carries no runtime meaning - it is
// accepted and dropped (it pushes nothing, so the enclosing item list simply skips it).
// It does switch multicast combination on (csHasDeleg), so a program that declares no
// delegate type emits exactly the IR it emitted before.
DelegateDecl <~~ csNoteDeleg() ~~>
            = LeadMods KwDelegate Type Id [ SkipAngle ] SkipParen ";" ;

// A non-class type declared inside a class body: accepted, reported as not implemented; it
// fires notImpl and pushes nothing, so makeClassStmt simply skips it.
NestedUnsup <~~ notImpl("nested type", up.pos) ~~>
            = LeadMods NestedKw Id [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" Type { "," Type } ] SkipBlock ;
NestedKw    = ( "record" ) KwEnd ;

// C# identifiers are UNICODE (ECMA-334 7.4.3: an identifier may contain any
// Unicode letter, and Ändern / 쯠쯡쯢 / Fo\u203fo are all legal names), so this
// grammar carries its OWN Id and KwEnd instead of including lib/ident.abnf -
// that fragment is shared by a dozen grammars and must stay ASCII. The range
// starts at \x7f rather than \x80 on purpose: \x80 is a UTF-8 CONTINUATION byte
// and a range starting there matches nothing at all (docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas).
Id          = IdStart :whitespace() { IdPart } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
IdStart     = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" | "\x7f"..."\U0010ffff" ;
IdPart      = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" | "\x7f"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// The keyword boundary counts a non-ASCII byte as a word character too, so
// `newÄ` stays one identifier rather than `new` followed by a name.
KwEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        return ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || cc === 95
                || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc >= 127)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01keyword-boundary", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

KwUsing     = "using" KwEnd ;
KwNamespace = "namespace" KwEnd ;
KwEnum      = "enum" KwEnd ;
KwDelegate  = "delegate" KwEnd ;
KwTry       = "try" KwEnd ;
KwCatch     = "catch" KwEnd ;
KwFinally   = "finally" KwEnd ;
KwWhen      = "when" KwEnd ;
KwThrow     = "throw" KwEnd ;
KwIf        = "if" KwEnd ;
KwElse      = "else" KwEnd ;
KwWhile     = "while" KwEnd ;
KwDo        = "do" KwEnd ;
KwFor       = "for" KwEnd ;
KwForeach   = "foreach" KwEnd ;
KwIn        = "in" KwEnd ;
KwReturn    = "return" KwEnd ;
KwBreak     = "break" KwEnd ;
KwContinue  = "continue" KwEnd ;
KwNew       = "new" KwEnd ;
KwBase      = "base" KwEnd ;
KwThis      = "this" KwEnd ;
KwSwitch    = "switch" KwEnd ;
KwCase      = "case" KwEnd ;
KwGoto      = "goto" KwEnd ;
KwDefault   = "default" KwEnd ;
KwFrom      = "from" KwEnd ;
KwSelect    = "select" KwEnd ;
KwWhere     = "where" KwEnd ;
KwOrderby   = "orderby" KwEnd ;
KwStackalloc = "stackalloc" KwEnd ;
KwGroup     = "group" KwEnd ;
KwBy        = "by" KwEnd ;
KwJoin      = "join" KwEnd ;
KwOn        = "on" KwEnd ;
KwEquals    = "equals" KwEnd ;
KwInto      = "into" KwEnd ;
KwLet       = "let" KwEnd ;

// ---


:startScript(~~

    // Shared emitter machinery (module, handle constants, externs, loopStack protocol,
    // expression/statement builders); see the `core` knobs below.
    include("lib/compile-core.js")

    var feCount = 0 // Names hidden foreach counters.

    // ----- Imports & errors -----
    function fail(msg) { println("csharp compiler error: " + msg); exit(1) }

    // Import roots the runtime already provides (Console, collections, ...); importing
    // them is a no-op. Anything else is a library we cannot resolve.
    core.stdlibImports = ["System", "Microsoft"]

    // Project-file imports: 'using A.B.C' looks for A/B/C.cs, then A/B.cs under the
    // program's directory and the -i roots. The file is parsed with this grammar; its
    // top-level declarations are stashed by buildMain (importDepth > 0) and prepended to
    // jsmain. A file is imported once; repeats and cycles are no-ops.
    var importedFiles = {}
    var importDepth = 0
    // imported files top-level items: stashImported / emitImported (compile-core)
    core.importFile = function(path, pos) {
        var rel = path.split(".").join("/")
        var cands = [rel + ".cs"]
        var cut = -1
        for (var k = 0; k < rel.length; k++) { if (rel.charAt(k) == "/") { cut = k } }
        if (cut > 0) { cands.push(rel.substring(0, cut) + ".cs") }
        for (var i = 0; i < cands.length; i++) {
            var file = c.findImport(cands[i])
            if (file == "") { continue }
            if (importedFiles["$" + file] == true) { return true }
            importedFiles["$" + file] = true
            var text = c.readFile(file)
            var asg = c.parse(c.agrammar, text, null, file)
            c.pushSource(file, text)
            importDepth = importDepth + 1
            c.compile(asg)
            importDepth = importDepth - 1
            c.popSource()
            return true
        }
        return false
    }

    // Index reads (arr[i], dict[k], s[i]) go through js_pyget (array/dict/string aware).
    core.indexExt = "js_pyget"

    // The bitwise / shift operators reuse the runtime externals (they already truncate to
    // 32 bit through wrapNum(toInt32(...)), so they need no extra intWrap). Added to the
    // shared binExt table so foldBinary and the compound assignments emit them directly.
    // NOT the runtime's 32 bit externs any more: js_bor/js_band/js_shl are
    // JavaScript's THIRTY-TWO bit operators, so 1L << 40 answered 256 and
    // uint.MaxValue >> 1 answered -1 >> 1. The emitBin override below routes
    // every one of them through js_csarith / js_csshift, which apply the
    // ECMA-334 12.4.7.3 promotion and mask the shift count.

    // == / != go through the char-aware equality (js_jchareq is js_seq unless a char meets
    // the one-character string it renders as).
    // C#'s '+': "x" + null is "x", not "xnull" - the js_csadd external, which is
    // js_jadd with C#'s null rendering (and the same double awareness).
    binExt["+"] = "js_csadd2"
    binExt["+="] = "js_csadd2"
    var coreEmitBin = emitBin
    emitBin = function(b, op, l, r) {
        if (op == "==" || op == "!=") {
            var eqv = anytype
            // A program that carries a record or a tuple compares them BY VALUE, and does
            // so recursively (a record field that is itself a record or a tuple compares
            // by value too). Every other program keeps the plain char-aware identity test.
            if (csHasValEq) {
                var ar = callExt(b, "js_arr_new", [])
                callExt(b, "js_arr_push", [ar, l])
                callExt(b, "js_arr_push", [ar, r])
                eqv = callExt(b, "js_call", [callExt(b, "js_scope_get", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, "$valeq")]), hUndef, ar])
            } else {
                eqv = callExt(b, "js_jchareq", [l, r])
            }
            // Two INTEGRAL operands compare BY VALUE at their promoted type:
            // without this a `long x = 5` box and the plain int 5 are two
            // different runtime values and `x == 5` is false.
            eqv = callExt(b, "js_cseq", [l, r, eqv])
            if (op == "!=") { return callExt(b, "js_not", [eqv]) }
            return eqv
        }
        // ----- double / float / decimal: a boxed js_csflo value (see jsrtjvm.go) -----
        // C#'s double is a DIFFERENT type from int, and the handle runtime models every
        // number as one JS double, so 1.0 / 3.0 and 1 / 3 arrived here as the same two
        // operands. Every arithmetic operator was followed by the shared intWrap, which
        // made the float half of the language silently integer arithmetic: 2.5 * 1.5 was
        // 3, 1.0 / 3.0 was 0 and Infinity / NaN did not exist. The js_jf* externs answer
        // a double whenever one operand is one and keep the old int32 wrap otherwise -
        // this is what csharp-interpreter.abnf's {__flo} box does.
        // The string CONVERSION of an operand of '+'. js_csadd on its own renders a
        // bool as JavaScript's "true"/"false" (C# writes "True"/"False"), an array
        // as its elements and a class instance as [object Object]. js_csstr
        // converts exactly those three shapes - and honours a user ToString()
        // override - and passes every other value through UNCHANGED, so two ints
        // still add as ints and a boxed double still adds as a double.
        if (op == "+" || op == "+=") {
            return callExt(b, "js_csadd2", [callExt(b, "js_csstr", [l]), callExt(b, "js_csstr", [r])])
        }
        // Every INTEGRAL operator routes through js_csarith / js_csshift /
        // js_cscmp, which apply the ECMA-334 12.4.7.3 promotion (ulong / long /
        // uint / int) and wrap at the promoted type - so the whole width story
        // lives in one place, exactly as csArith does in csharp-interpreter.abnf.
        if (op == "-" || op == "-=") return callExt(b, "js_csarith", [emitStr(b, "-"), l, r])
        if (op == "*" || op == "*=") return callExt(b, "js_csarith", [emitStr(b, "*"), l, r])
        if (op == "/" || op == "/=") return callExt(b, "js_csarith", [emitStr(b, "/"), l, r])
        if (op == "%" || op == "%=") return callExt(b, "js_csarith", [emitStr(b, "%"), l, r])
        if (op == "&" || op == "&=") return callExt(b, "js_csarith", [emitStr(b, "&"), l, r])
        if (op == "|" || op == "|=") return callExt(b, "js_csarith", [emitStr(b, "|"), l, r])
        if (op == "^" || op == "^=") return callExt(b, "js_csarith", [emitStr(b, "^"), l, r])
        // The shift COUNT is masked - & 31 for an int/uint, & 63 for a long/ulong
        // - and the result type is the LEFT operand's alone (ECMA-334 12.11), so
        // `1 << 32` is 1; `>>` on an unsigned type is the LOGICAL shift.
        if (op == "<<" || op == "<<=") return callExt(b, "js_csshift", [emitStr(b, "<<"), l, r])
        if (op == ">>" || op == ">>=") return callExt(b, "js_csshift", [emitStr(b, ">>"), l, r])
        if (op == ">>>" || op == ">>>=") return callExt(b, "js_csshift", [emitStr(b, ">>>"), l, r])
        if (op == "<") return callExt(b, "js_cscmp", [emitStr(b, "<"), l, r])
        if (op == ">") return callExt(b, "js_cscmp", [emitStr(b, ">"), l, r])
        if (op == "<=") return callExt(b, "js_cscmp", [emitStr(b, "<="), l, r])
        if (op == ">=") return callExt(b, "js_cscmp", [emitStr(b, ">="), l, r])
        return coreEmitBin(b, op, l, r)
    }

    // Binary and unary steps route through the operator dispatch (emitBinDyn / emitUnDyn),
    // which is the plain built-in emission when the program declares no such operator.
    foldBinary = function(items) {
        if (items.length == 1) { return items[0] }
        return function(b) {
            var r = items[0](b)
            b = r.b
            var v = r.v
            for (var i = 1; i < items.length; i += 2) {
                var rr = items[i + 1](b)
                b = rr.b
                var d = emitBinDyn(b, items[i], v, rr.v)
                b = d.b
                v = d.v
            }
            return {b: b, v: v}
        }
    }
    makeNeg = function(t) {
        return function(b) {
            var r = t(b)
            return emitUnDyn(r.b, "-", r.v, function(bb) { return callExt(bb, "js_csneg", [r.v]) })
        }
    }
    makeNot = function(t) {
        return function(b) {
            var r = t(b)
            return emitUnDyn(r.b, "!", r.v, function(bb) { return callExt(bb, "js_not", [r.v]) })
        }
    }

    // ~v is the complement at the UNARY PROMOTED type (ECMA-334 12.4.7.2): int
    // for a byte/sbyte/short/ushort/char/int, and the operand's own type for
    // uint/long/ulong. `v ^ -1` through js_bxor was always the 32 bit signed one.
    function makeBitNot(t) {
        return function(b) {
            var r = t(b)
            b = r.b
            return {b: b, v: callExt(b, "js_csnot", [r.v])}
        }
    }
    // 'a ?? b': the first operand that is not null wins; the right side runs only when the
    // left is null (js_eq is the loose test, so undefined counts as null too).
    function csCoalesce2(lt, rt) {
        return function(b) {
            var r = lt(b)
            b = r.b
            var thenB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var elseB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var mergeB = curF.NewBlock("")
            b.NewCondBr(truthy(b, callExt(b, "js_eq", [r.v, hNull])), thenB, elseB)
            var rr = rt(thenB)
            rr.b.NewBr(mergeB)
            elseB.NewBr(mergeB)
            return {b: mergeB, v: mergeB.NewPhi([llvm.ir.NewIncoming(rr.v, rr.b), llvm.ir.NewIncoming(r.v, elseB)])}
        }
    }
    function makeNullCoal(items, pos) {
        if (items.length == 1) { return items[0] }
        var t = items[0]
        for (var i = 1; i < items.length; i++) { t = csCoalesce2(t, items[i]) }
        return t
    }
    // The C# type name a js_is_type probe understands: the runtime speaks the shared value
    // model's names ("Int", "String", "Any", ...), so the primitives are mapped here, at
    // compile time; a user type keeps its own simple name.
    function csIrType(t0) {
        var t = csSimpleName(t0)
        var opt = ""
        if (t.length > 0 && t.charAt(t.length - 1) == "?") { t = t.substring(0, t.length - 1); opt = "?" }
        if (t == "object" || t == "dynamic" || t == "var") { return "Any" + opt }
        if (t == "string" || t == "String") { return "String" + opt }
        if (t == "bool") { return "Boolean" + opt }
        if (t == "char") { return "Char" + opt }
        if (t == "int" || t == "uint" || t == "long" || t == "ulong" || t == "short"
            || t == "ushort" || t == "byte" || t == "sbyte") { return "Int" + opt }
        if (t == "double" || t == "float" || t == "decimal") { return "Double" + opt }
        if (t == "IList" || t == "IEnumerable" || t == "ICollection") { return "List" + opt }
        return t + opt
    }
    // ----- patterns -----
    // Every pattern production builds one record - the same shape the interpreter grammar
    // builds - and emitPat lowers that record to a test. A binding is declared as soon as
    // its own sub-test is reached: C# forbids reading a pattern variable on a path where
    // the pattern did not match, so what it holds after a failed test is unobservable.
    function csPatOr(items) { return items.length == 1 ? items[0] : {k: "or", ps: items} }
    function csPatAnd(items) { return items.length == 1 ? items[0] : {k: "and", ps: items} }
    function csPatNot(p) { return {k: "not", p: p} }
    function csPatNull() { return {k: "null"} }
    function csPatDiscard() { return {k: "discard"} }
    function csPatRel(items) { return {k: "rel", op: items[0], t: items[1]} }
    function csPatConst(t) { return {k: "const", t: t} }
    function csPatVar(name) { return {k: "var", name: name} }
    function csPatSub(items) { return {subname: items[0], subpat: items[1]} }
    function csPatSlice(items) {
        var s = {k: "slice", p: null}
        if (items.length > 0) { s.p = items[0] }
        return s
    }
    function csPatType(items) {
        var p = {k: "type", ty: "", bind: ""}
        for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
            if (items[i].patty != undefined) { p.ty = items[i].patty; continue }
            if (items[i].patbind != undefined) { p.bind = items[i].patbind }
        }
        return p
    }
    function csPatProp(items) {
        var p = {k: "prop", ty: "", bind: "", subs: []}
        var subs = []
        for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
            var it = items[i]
            if (it.patty != undefined) { p.ty = it.patty; continue }
            if (it.patbind != undefined) { p.bind = it.patbind; continue }
            subs.push(it)
        }
        p.subs = subs
        return p
    }
    function csPatPos(items) {
        var p = {k: "pos", ty: "", bind: "", subs: []}
        var subs = []
        for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
            var it = items[i]
            if (it.patty != undefined) { p.ty = it.patty; continue }
            if (it.patbind != undefined) { p.bind = it.patbind; continue }
            subs.push(it)
        }
        p.subs = subs
        return p
    }
    function csPatList(items) {
        var p = {k: "list", bind: "", elems: []}
        var el = []
        for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
            if (items[i].patbind != undefined) { p.bind = items[i].patbind; continue }
            el.push(items[i])
        }
        p.elems = el
        return p
    }

    // A short-circuiting conjunction / disjunction of sub-tests. Every failing (winning)
    // sub-test branches to ONE shared block, so the merge phi has exactly two incomings
    // however many sub-tests there are.
    function emitAllOf(b, fns) {
        if (fns.length == 0) { return {b: b, v: hTrue} }
        if (fns.length == 1) { return fns[0](b) }
        var falseB = curF.NewBlock("")
        var mergeB = curF.NewBlock("")
        var cur = b
        for (var i = 0; i < fns.length; i++) {
            var r = fns[i](cur)
            cur = r.b
            var nb = curF.NewBlock("")
            cur.NewCondBr(truthy(cur, r.v), nb, falseB)
            cur = nb
        }
        cur.NewBr(mergeB)
        falseB.NewBr(mergeB)
        return {b: mergeB, v: mergeB.NewPhi([llvm.ir.NewIncoming(hTrue, cur), llvm.ir.NewIncoming(hFalse, falseB)])}
    }
    function emitAnyOf(b, fns) {
        if (fns.length == 0) { return {b: b, v: hFalse} }
        if (fns.length == 1) { return fns[0](b) }
        var trueB = curF.NewBlock("")
        var mergeB = curF.NewBlock("")
        var cur = b
        for (var i = 0; i < fns.length; i++) {
            var r = fns[i](cur)
            cur = r.b
            var nb = curF.NewBlock("")
            cur.NewCondBr(truthy(cur, r.v), trueB, nb)
            cur = nb
        }
        cur.NewBr(mergeB)
        trueB.NewBr(mergeB)
        return {b: mergeB, v: mergeB.NewPhi([llvm.ir.NewIncoming(hFalse, cur), llvm.ir.NewIncoming(hTrue, trueB)])}
    }
    // The sub-test closures. Each is built by its own function so it captures its own
    // operands rather than a shared loop variable.
    function csPatFn(p, v) { return function(b) { return emitPat(b, p, v) } }
    function csNotNullFn(v) {
        return function(b) { return {b: b, v: callExt(b, "js_not", [callExt(b, "js_eq", [v, hNull])])} }
    }
    function csTypeFn(ty, v) {
        return function(b) { return {b: b, v: callExt(b, "js_is_type", [v, emitStr(b, ty)])} }
    }
    function csBindFn(name, v) {
        return function(b) {
            callExt(b, "js_scope_decl", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, name), v])
            return {b: b, v: hTrue}
        }
    }
    // A property-pattern member read. It uses the same mapping the '.name' suffix of a
    // member chain does, so '{ Length: 3 }' works on a string / array / list too.
    function csPropFn(name, sub, v) {
        return function(b) {
            var fv = anytype
            if (name == "Count" || name == "Length") { fv = callExt(b, "js_cslen", [v]) }
            else if (csPropAcc[name] != undefined) { fv = emitPropGet(b, v, name) }
            else { fv = callExt(b, "js_get", [v, emitStr(b, name)]) }
            return emitPat(b, sub, fv)
        }
    }
    function csItemFn(tv, i, sub) {
        return function(b) { return emitPat(b, sub, callExt(b, "js_get", [tv, emitStr(b, "Item" + (i + 1))])) }
    }
    function csPosFn(p, v) {
        return function(b) {
            var t = csDeconValue(b, v)
            b = t.b
            var subs = p.subs
            var fs = []
            for (var i = 0; i < subs.length; i++) { fs.push(csItemFn(t.v, i, subs[i])) }
            if (p.bind != "") { fs.push(csBindFn(p.bind, v)) }
            return emitAllOf(b, fs)
        }
    }
    function csLenFn(v, exact, n) {
        return function(b) {
            var lv = callExt(b, "js_cslen", [v])
            if (exact) { return {b: b, v: callExt(b, "js_seq", [lv, emitNum(b, n)])} }
            return {b: b, v: callExt(b, "js_ge", [lv, emitNum(b, n)])}
        }
    }
    function csElemFn(v, i, sub) {
        return function(b) { return emitPat(b, sub, callExt(b, "js_pyget", [v, emitNum(b, i)])) }
    }
    // An element counted from the END: index length - k.
    function csTailElemFn(v, k, sub) {
        return function(b) {
            var iv = callExt(b, "js_sub", [callExt(b, "js_cslen", [v]), emitNum(b, k)])
            return emitPat(b, sub, callExt(b, "js_pyget", [v, iv]))
        }
    }
    function csSliceFn(v, head, tail, sub) {
        return function(b) {
            var hi = callExt(b, "js_sub", [callExt(b, "js_cslen", [v]), emitNum(b, tail)])
            return emitPat(b, sub, callExt(b, "js_goslice", [v, emitNum(b, head), hi]))
        }
    }
    function emitPosPat(b, p, v) {
        var fp = []
        fp.push(csNotNullFn(v))
        if (p.ty != "") { fp.push(csTypeFn(csIrType(p.ty), v)) }
        fp.push(csPosFn(p, v))
        return emitAllOf(b, fp)
    }
    function emitListPat(b, p, v) {
        var elems = p.elems
        var i
        var si = -1
        for (i = 0; i < elems.length; i++) { if (elems[i].k == "slice") { si = i } }
        var fl = []
        fl.push(csTypeFn("List", v))
        if (si < 0) {
            fl.push(csLenFn(v, true, elems.length))
            for (i = 0; i < elems.length; i++) { fl.push(csElemFn(v, i, elems[i])) }
        } else {
            var head = si
            var tail = elems.length - si - 1
            fl.push(csLenFn(v, false, head + tail))
            for (i = 0; i < head; i++) { fl.push(csElemFn(v, i, elems[i])) }
            for (i = 0; i < tail; i++) { fl.push(csTailElemFn(v, tail - i, elems[si + 1 + i])) }
            var sub = elems[si].p
            if (sub != null && sub != undefined) { fl.push(csSliceFn(v, head, tail, sub)) }
